em and from  beginning to end I want the reading  experience to be simple but when they  finish that poem I want  our stomach to turn and then I want them  to sit with that emotion and really work  on that women of color our backs tell  stories that no books have the spine to  carry do you see a lot of women like  yourself and I mean South Asian women  women of Indian descent in art  entertainment on the spines of books I  see it happening more anymore I will say  that when I was growing up they were  very very few and I actually didn't know  any of them because I didn't even have  access to them and they weren't you know  front and center in my face sort of  thing although they were there you know  when I was designing milk and honey it  was so important to see the word core  right on this mind I didn't even want my  first name on it  I just every sick woman has that name  right and so I was like when I want a  seven-year-old to go into a bookstore  and see her name on a buck because I  never got that growing up and it's like  if she sees that she'll be able to see  that she can do it too  so you heard what she said she says  every sick woman has that name core she  means like or seek with the Sikh  religion but suppose it's properly  pronounced Sikh religion has the name  core and so she credits her faith really  with giving her that inspiration she  says it means a great deal that she's  now inspiring a lot of dumb young women  of all different kinds of face to see  their name in print - I love that she's  25 and I love the realization that she  had about her own mother and father it's  interesting as a kid you see them  differently when you grow up and think  you know they're not so bad they're  actually kind of smart I'm very  personable she is finding both her voice  and and that's also a part of culture  too because there are a lot of  gatekeepers in poetry as well who want  to keep out it she went around that yeah  it's really her art I like the message  tha